<p>Boooring. The article's written pretty well, I think, nothing to object. It's the very <em>idea</em> of it to be boring, I guess: an ancient but technologically advanced weapon? Meh. Not a <em>very</em> bad idea, but you could arrange something more original and supernatural than &quot;lasers&quot;. Plus, some kind of lasers <em>actually</em> existed in ancient Greece: large, circular, concave mirrors which reflected and focused solar rays upon ships and set them on fire. This means that not only it's not very interesting (in which case I would just have left no vote), but also it's neither very anomalous itself, nor historically plausible (who needs super-duper-complex machinery when you've got something equally effective, but far less difficult to create, build or understand?).<sup class="footnoteref"><a id="footnoteref-649538-1" href="javascript:;" class="footnoteref" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnote-649538-1')">1</a></sup><br />
No mirror has been armed during the production of this post.</p>
